a. the range coder is symmetric
   every EncodeBit(prob, bit) has exactly one matching
   DecodeBit(prob) that returns the same bit

b. the probability tables are updated identically
   encoder updates prob after every EncodeBit
   decoder updates prob after every DecodeBit
   same update formula, same order, same entries

c. the token sequence is preserved
   every literal and match encoded produces
   exactly the bits needed to reconstruct it

a. range never reaches 0
   bound = (range >> 11) * prob
   prob is always in [1, 2047]
   range >= (1<<24) after normalization
   therefore bound >= (range>>11) * 1 > 0
   therefore range never collapses to 0

b. low never overflows past the interval
   low + range <= 0xFFFFFFFF always holds
   ShiftLow correctly handles carries
   output bytes are committed only when stable

c. normalization preserves the interval
   range <<= 8 and ShiftLow are equivalent to
   multiplying both low and range by 256
   the relative position inside the interval is unchanged

after seeing n zeros and m ones the probability satisfies:

prob_n ≈ 2048 * (32/(32+1))^m * product_of_zero_updates

more simply — the effective window is ~32 samples
prob converges to true_freq within O(32) samples

formally:
  if P(bit=0) = p in the data
  then E[prob] → 2048 * p  as n → infinity

prove: E[prob | bit=0] = prob + (2048-prob)/32
       E[prob | bit=1] = prob - prob/32
       fixed point when E[prob] = 2048*p:
         p*(2048 - 2048*p)/32 = (1-p)*(2048*p)/32
         2048*p*(1-p)/32 = 2048*p*(1-p)/32  ✓



a. range coder achieves near-Shannon entropy
   each bit costs log2(1/p) bits where p is the
   current probability estimate
   total cost = sum of log2(1/p_i) for all bits

b. greedy match finder suboptimality
   greedy selection is at most O(log N) bits
   worse than optimal parsing
   proof: each greedy decision can be at most
   one match length shorter than optimal
   optimal parser would use price model to find
   globally optimal token sequence

c. probability model overhead
   initial 1024 probabilities cost exactly 1 bit per decision
   overhead = O(32) bits per probability entry to converge
   total overhead = O(K * 32) where K = number of prob entries
   K = 6144 + 192 + 12 + 256 + 16 + 514 ≈ 7034 entries
   overhead ≈ 225088 bits ≈ 27 KB regardless of input size



a. chain integrity
   every position inserted maintains the invariant:
   chain[pos % dictSize] points to the previous position
   with the same 2-byte hash, or 0xFFFFFFFF if none

   proof by induction:
   base: all entries 0xFFFFFFFF initially
   step: each FindMatch call sets:
     hashTable[h] = pos
     chain[pos % dictSize] = old_head
   this correctly prepends pos to the chain for hash h

b. completeness up to depth
   all candidates within depth=32 steps of the chain
   are checked — no valid match is skipped within
   the search window

c. distance bound
   dist = pos - cur
   if dist > dictSize → break
   proves no match outside the dictionary window
   is ever returned

d. length correctness
   the byte comparison loop is exact:
   while len < maxLen && src[pos+len] == src[cur+len]
   returns exactly the longest common prefix
   between src[pos..] and src[cur..]


